a the&nbsp;<strong>One Big Beautiful Bill / One Big Beautiful Bill Act</strong>), signed into law&nbsp;<strong>July 4, 2025</strong>, the FICA tip credit was expanded to include&nbsp;<strong>beauty service establishments</strong>.&nbsp;</p><p>The bill language extends ┬º45B to ΓÇ£beauty servicesΓÇ¥ and applies to&nbsp;<strong>tax years beginning after Dec. 31, 2024</strong>&nbsp;(meaning itΓÇÖs claimed with&nbsp;<strong>2025 returns</strong>).&nbsp;</p><p><strong>Beauty services (as defined in the bill / IRS form instructions) include:</strong></p><ul><li>Barbering &amp; hair care</li><li>Nail care</li><li>Esthetics</li><li>Body &amp; spa treatments&nbsp;</li></ul><p><strong>Important beauty-industry qualifier:</strong></p><p>To claim the credit for beauty services, the law adds a threshold:&nbsp;<strong>tips for those services must exceed 15% of gross receipts</strong>&nbsp;for those services in the year.&nbsp;</p><p><strong>Beauty minimum wage basis for the credit:</strong>&nbsp;Form 8846 instructions reference&nbsp;<strong>$7.25/hour</strong>&nbsp;for beauty service employees.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Γ£¿ Qualifying Beauty Businesses Where Tipping Is Standard</strong></h3><p>If tipping is customary and you employ W-2 staff, many of these often fall into the ΓÇ£beauty servicesΓÇ¥ bucket (or contain qualifying services inside the business):</p><ul><li>Hair salons &amp; barbershops (stylists, colorists, barbers, assistants/shampoo staff)</li><li>Nail salons (manicures/pedicures)</li><li>Spas / wellness centers (facials, wraps, body treatments, etc.)</li><li>Esthetics &amp; skincare studios (waxing, lashes, brows, tanning, etc.)</li><li>Makeup artists (studio/event work)</li></ul><p><strong>Note:</strong>&nbsp;Tipping is generally not expected/allowed for medical professionals performing medical procedures (Botox/fillers), but&nbsp;<strong>non-medical services</strong>&nbsp;in the same facility may still involve tips and may qualify depending on service type.</p>',1,199,1,'2026-02-18 17:55:52','2026-02-18 17:55:52'),(1159,'Easily Explained','<p><strong>ΓÇ£Dan was the first person who explained this credit in plain English and then actually did the work. Eligibility, tip rules, documentationΓÇöhandled. We stopped guessing and started filing correctly.ΓÇ¥</strong></p><p>Anthony R., Restaurant Owner ΓÇö Los Angeles, CA</p>',1,199,3,'2026-02-18 17:55:52','2026-02-18 20:25:11'),(1160,'Found What We Missed','<p><strong>ΓÇ£We assumed payroll or our CPA would automatically catch this. Nope. Dan found what we missed, showed us why, and packaged it in a way that felt clean and defensible.ΓÇ¥</strong></p><p>Melissa K., Controller ΓÇö San Diego, CA</p>',1,199,3,'2026-02-18 17:55:52','2026-02-18 20:25:33'),(1161,'Tight Process','<p><strong>ΓÇ£His process is tight: tip vs service charge separation, minimum wage threshold math, and making sure everything aligns with payroll reporting. No fluffΓÇöjust results.ΓÇ¥</strong></p><p>Derek L., Bar &amp; Lounge Operator ΓÇö San Jose, CA</p>',1,199,3,'2026-02-18 17:55:52','2026-02-18 20:25:58'),(1162,'Knows Her Craft','<p><strong>ΓÇ£Dan doesnΓÇÖt ΓÇÿkind ofΓÇÖ understand the FICA tip creditΓÇöhe knows it cold. He caught mistakes we didnΓÇÖt even realize mattered and helped us fix them going forward.ΓÇ¥</strong></p><p>Priya S., Hospitality Owner ΓÇö San Francisco, CA</p>',1,199,3,'2026-02-18 17:55:52','2026-02-18 20:26:29'),(1163,'Understands Your Need','<p><strong>ΓÇ£WeΓÇÖre in California and the wage/tip rules can get complicated fast. Dan knew exactly how to navigate it and make sure we only claimed what truly qualified.ΓÇ¥</strong></p><p>Jonathan M., Multi-Location Operator ΓÇö Sacramento, CA</p>',1,199,3,'2026-02-18 17:55:52','2026-02-18 20:26:46'),(1164,'Walked Us Through With Ease','<p><strong>ΓÇ£WeΓÇÖre a salon with W-2 staff and didnΓÇÖt realize we could even be in the conversation. Dan walked us through the requirements, confirmed what counted, and made the process simple.ΓÇ¥</strong><